</section>

<section id="definitions">
  <h2>Nested definitions</h2>
  <p>
    The shared lifecycle definitions. Every v0.4 contract that models
    lifecycle metadata <code>$ref</code>s the ones it needs from here, so
    all entity types share one vocabulary.
  </p>
  <div class="schema-definition-list">
    <article class="schema-definition">
      <h3 id="definition-Status"><code>Status</code></h3>
      {{ definitionIntro('Status') }}
      <p>
        A plain <code>{{ ref.definitions.Status.type }}</code> with no
        constraints. Consumers may use any value; the lifecycle contract
        treats only <code>deprecated</code> and <code>removed</code> as
        normative, and everything else is informational.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="schema-definition">
      <h3 id="definition-Deprecated"><code>Deprecated</code></h3>
      {{ definitionIntro('Deprecated') }}
      <p>
        This definition is permissive: unknown properties are allowed, so
        consumers can extend records without breaking validation.
      </p>
      {{ fieldTable(ref.definitions.Deprecated.properties, 'Deprecated', 'v04Lifecycle') }}
    </article>

    <article class="schema-definition">
      <h3 id="definition-StrictDeprecated"><code>StrictDeprecated</code></h3>
      {{ definitionIntro('StrictDeprecated') }}
      <p>
        Same fields as <a href="#definition-Deprecated"><code>Deprecated</code></a>,
        but closed: <code>additionalProperties: false</code> rejects unknown
        keys, keeping the DTCG extension namespace strict.
      </p>
      {{ fieldTable(ref.definitions.StrictDeprecated.properties, 'StrictDeprecated', 'v04Lifecycle') }}
    </article>

    <article class="schema-definition">
      <h3 id="definition-NativeDeprecation"><code>NativeDeprecation</code></h3>
      {{ definitionIntro('NativeDeprecation') }}
      <p>A <code>oneOf</code> union over exactly three shapes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <code>boolean false</code>
          — an explicit "not deprecated" assertion in a native manifest.
          DTCG source cancellation uses the standard
          <code>$deprecated: false</code> field, not an extension value.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>null</code>
          — not asserted; no lifecycle claim.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="schema-ref" href="#definition-Deprecated"><code>Deprecated</code></a>
          — the full deprecation record.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The <code>false</code> and <code>null</code> branches keep the field
        lightweight when an entity is simply not deprecated, while the
        record branch carries the full deprecation payload.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="schema-definition">
      <h3 id="definition-DeprecatedValue"><code>DeprecatedValue</code></h3>
      {{ definitionIntro('DeprecatedValue') }}
      <p>
        Like <a href="#definition-Deprecated"><code>Deprecated</code></a> this
        definition is permissive on unknown properties, and it adds the
        required <code>value</code> field that names the deprecated
        attribute value.
      </p>
      {{ fieldTable(ref.definitions.DeprecatedValue.properties, 'DeprecatedValue', 'v04Lifecycle') }}
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

